- Janna Zervas (Juan Zaino)

Result: Update Household Owner to Juan Zaino.
Additional Update Candidates
JVN Restoration INC HH
Current Household Owner: Charles Marchica
Related Account Owner: James Pallitto III

Wellington Construction Group - HH (ACE)
Current Household Owner: Lily Rojas
Related Account Owner: Elizabeth Cabrera

SOUTH FLORIDA STUDIO, LLC - HH
Current Household Owner: Alejandro Gonzalez
Related Account Owner: Leslie Fischman
Proposed Solution
Monthly Scheduled Batch Apex

The batch starts from Household Accounts, so every Household is always evaluated with all of its related Accounts. This avoids a Household being split across batch chunks.

start(): query all Household Accounts (Household Record Type).
execute() (batch size 100-200):
Query related Accounts where CRM_Customer_Household__c IN :householdIds.
Build the set of distinct owners per Household.
If there is exactly one distinct owner and it differs from the Household Owner, add the Household to the update list.
Skip Households with no related Accounts or with multiple owners.
Update using Database.update(list, false) so one failure does not block the rest.
Write one audit log entry per update (Household Id, old owner, new owner, run date) and log all failures.
finish(): send a summary (updated, skipped, failed) to administrators.
Technical Components
Batch Apex class
Schedulable class (cron 0 0 2 1 * ?, 1st of every month at 2 AM)
Audit log (custom object or equivalent)
Custom Metadata toggle to enable or disable the job without a deployment
Dry-run mode (report only, no updates) for the first run
Test class
Processing Logic
IF
    Related Account Count > 0
    AND Distinct Owner Count = 1
    AND Household Owner != Related Account Owner
THEN
    Update Household Owner
ELSE
    Skip
Business Rules

Update Household Owner when:

Household contains one or more related Accounts (pending business confirmation for Households with only one related Account).
All related Accounts have the same Owner.
Household Owner differs from the common Owner.

Do not update when:

No related Accounts exist.
Related Accounts have multiple Owners.
Household Owner already matches the common Owner.
The common Owner is inactive or a queue (pending business confirmation; recommended: skip and log).
Risks and Considerations
Risk	Mitigation
Changing the Household Owner may trigger sharing recalculation and Relationship Team changes	Test in sandbox; review sharing rules and team setup
Existing Flows, Triggers or integrations on Account Owner change may fire	Review all automation on Account owner change before build
Record locking (UNABLE_TO_LOCK_ROW)	Small batch size (100-200), partial-success updates, retry failures
Inactive or queue owners	Skip and log
FSC roll-ups to the Household may be affected by an owner change	Validate in sandbox
Large first run (many mismatches at once)	Run in dry-run mode first and review the count with the business
Out of Scope
Households with multiple Relationship Owners (to be reviewed as a future enhancement, including additional backend ownership logic).
Ownership hierarchy logic.
Manager-based ownership assignment.
Real-time ownership synchronization.
Manual ownership conflict resolution.
Estimate and Next Steps

Estimate: 5-8 story points, depending on the automation found on Account Owner change.

Next steps:

Get business answers to the open questions below.
Review existing automation on Account Owner change.
Build the Batch class, Scheduler, audit log, toggle and test class.
Run the batch in dry-run mode in a sandbox and in Production. The dry run produces the exact number of Households that would be updated.
UAT with the business, then enable the live run.
Open Questions
Business Questions
Should the process overwrite a manually assigned Household Owner when all related Accounts have the same owner? (Recommended: yes, since the goal is to align the Household Owner with the Relationship Owner.)
If all related Accounts are owned by an inactive user, should the Household Owner still be updated? (Recommended: no, skip and log.)
Should ownership changes be reported or audited? (Recommended: yes, to support the goal of reducing ServiceNow tickets.)
Should notifications be sent when ownership changes occur?
Should Households with only one related Account be included?
Should the process run monthly only, or at a different frequency?
Technical Questions
Are there any Flows, Apex Triggers, Assignment Rules or Integrations that execute when Account Owner changes?
Should all Household records be processed or only active Households?
Is there any division or record type that should be excluded from processing? Please also confirm that “Customer (Household)”, “HH” and “HH (ACE)” all belong to the same Household Record Type.






